        type: 'quote',
        text: 'man is a giddy thing,\nand this is my conclusion',
        note: 'Benedick — Act 5, Scene 4. Benedick’s closing reflection accepts human inconsistency with affectionate humour and ends his arc with a joke at his own expense. After a play in which confident speeches about love and honour have repeatedly collapsed — his orchard soliloquy, Claudio’s denunciation, Leonato’s grief — his willingness to shrug at his own changeability offers a more generous model of selfhood than the rigid honour code that nearly destroyed Hero. Shakespeare gives the play’s moral last word to wit rather than heroic rhetoric, suggesting that a healthy society must learn to live with its own inconstancy.',
